Durarara!! AU
Chapter 25 – Part 1
Curtis made it into class after the bell had already decided he was late.
Long black braids shifted against his Raira uniform as he slid into his seat with the particular quiet of someone who had already put two men on a sidewalk and an open debt on Namie Yagiri before first period. The kit was still in his bag. The collar crease on a Yagiri coat was still in his hands if he thought about it. He did not think about it. He set his bag down, exhaled once through his nose, and let the classroom noise wash over the morning like it belonged to a different life.
Anri approached before he fully settled.
She had been watching the door. That much was obvious in the way she crossed the small space between their desks without waiting for the teacher to finish writing on the board — careful steps, bag held close, the same soft gravity she carried when something in her chest had been turning since a fight video and a dead phone line and an address sent in the dark.
“Curtis-kun.”
His name, quiet enough that only the nearest seats might catch it. She stopped beside his desk, not quite blocking the aisle, mismatched memory of the night he had vanished and the morning he had called her Anri-chan and told her to move on still sitting between them.
She took in his face — the same face, the same braids, no fresh blood, no obvious injury from whatever had made him late — and held her bag strap a little tighter.
“You’re… here,” she said, and then seemed to hear how thin it sounded. A small breath. “I mean. Good morning. You made it.”
A pause. The teacher’s chalk kept moving. Somewhere near the window Mikado’s attention had already flicked their way and then away again, jealousy filing another quiet note.
Anri’s voice dropped further.
“Are you all right? After… everything. You don’t have to say what happened this morning. I just—” She stopped, searchingly, the girl who apologized for existing still learning how to ask for a person without apologizing for the ask. “You look like you ran into something before class.”
She waited, standing in the narrow aisle with the whole complicated soft-landing thread in her hands, and for a moment the Namie debt and the scarred-throat tip and the two suits on the pavement sat one desk away from a girl who only wanted to know if the boy who kept disappearing was still reachable when the bell rang.
Curtis chuckled, low and easy, long black braids shifting as he tipped his head just enough to meet Anri’s eyes without making a scene in the aisle.
“Just some street punks.” The words came out light, almost bored with the morning already. “Don’t worry, Anri-chan. I promise I didn’t do what I did last time. We all walked away.”
It was half true in the way Curtis’s soft answers often were. The suits had walked eventually. Namie had walked with an open debt and a scar on a stranger’s neck living behind her eyes. No spine. No viral bucket. No crowd chanting a fake ring name. For Anri’s sake, the sidewalk became street punks and the debt became nothing she needed to carry into first period.
Anri searched his face.
The chuckle helped. The Anri-chan helped more. Some of the tightness in her shoulders eased, though not all of it — she had seen what “walked away” could look like when he was angry, and she had spent hours hunting a boy who would not answer his phone.
“That’s… good,” she said softly. “That everyone walked away.” A small pause. Her fingers flexed on her bag strap. “You still look like the morning got to you before the bell did. If the punks come back, or if it’s more than punks—”
She stopped herself, glancing once toward the front of the class so the teacher would not mark them both. When she looked back, the concern was still there, quieter.
“I’m glad you’re here. That’s all. You don’t have to tell me the rest.”
She lingered half a second longer, as if making sure the soft landing was still real, then slipped toward her own seat before the teacher turned. Across the room Mikado’s pen had gone still on the page. Rio, a few desks over, had caught the Anri-chan and filed it without comment.
Curtis faced forward, kit heavy in his bag, Namie’s acceptance still warm on the street behind him, and let the classroom pretend the most dangerous thing in his morning had been street punks who all walked away.
A few desks over, Rio smirked.
It was small, private, aimed at the aisle where Anri had just retreated from Curtis’s desk with that soft, careful orbit she always used when she was trying not to ask for too much. Anri-chan. Street punks. Everyone walked away. The kind of gentle deflection that made girls lean in without noticing they were leaning.
Rio’s thoughts stayed her own.
Talk all you want in the light.
That doesn’t mean I won’t see him at night before you do.
She did not know Anri had been at his place the night before — the address on a phone, the hours spent looking, the crack still waiting in a different pavement. That file was closed to her. She also did not know, and neither did Anri, that Celty’s helmet and Celty’s body and a roof and a bed had rewritten a different part of Curtis’s dark hours entirely. The dullahan stayed a shadow. The sex stayed a secret. The information silos held.
What Rio had was simpler and older: a boy on a rooftedge who had caught her instead of watching her fall. Braids in a street performance. A motorcycle helmet that still covered his face when he drove her home. Numbers exchanged. A flat he lived in alone. The version of Curtis that answered when the city got heavy after sunset.
She turned a page she was not reading, smirk fading into something more thoughtful than smug.
Daytime belonged to classrooms and class reps and soft voices in the aisle.
Night was still a free country.
And as far as Rio Kamichika knew, she had not lost her place in it just because Anri Sonohara got a don’t worry before first period.
By lunch the two of them reached him at almost the same time.
Rio from the left, already wearing that private smirk like she had claimed the hour in advance. Anri from the right, softer, bag held close, the morning’s Anri-chan and street-punks story still warming a careful hope in her chest. Both had the same sentence ready in different clothes:
Lunch with Curtis.
They stopped short when they realized the other was there. For a beat the aisle became a three-body problem — long black braids between a girl who had fallen from a roof into his arms and a girl who had watched him vanish after a street fight and still come looking.
“Curtis-kun,” Anri started.
“Hey,” Rio said, overlapping, chin tipping toward the door. “Cafeteria. Or outside. You’re free, right?”
Curtis looked up from the desk.
Whatever he had spent on Namie’s collar and two suits and an open debt before first period had collected interest. The night before — Celty, the roof, the bed, secrets neither girl in front of him owned — sat under his eyes in a way sleep had not fully erased. He blinked once, slow, and the classroom noise seemed to reach him from farther away than the hallway.
“Ah…” Long black braids shifted as he rubbed the heel of his hand against one eye. “You both—”
The lunch bell finished what the morning had started.
By the time the room began to empty, Curtis’s head had already started to tip. He tried to sit up for them. He managed a tired half-smile that was meant to be fair to both and landed as apology instead.
“Rain check,” he murmured. “I’m… not gonna make it past the door without face-planting. Sorry.”
He was asleep at his desk before the afternoon period fully claimed the room.
Head turned toward the window, braids spilling over one arm, breathing even in the way of someone who had decided the body got a vote. Rio stood over him for a second with her smirk gone flat. Anri hovered a step behind, fingers tight on her bag strap, the soft landing postponed again by a boy who could put men on pavement before school and still lose the war to sleep by lunch.
Neither of them knew about the debt in Namie’s pocket.
Neither of them knew about the dullahan in his bed.
They only knew the same problem in stereo:
Lunch with Curtis had turned into watching Curtis sleep, and the night — for whichever of them reached it first — was still unclaimed.
They stepped into the hallway and the door clicked shut on the sleeping shape of him — braids, desk, the soft rise and fall of a boy who had already lived a full street war before first period.
For a second neither spoke.
Then the looks met.
Rio’s carried the rooftop and the motorcycle and the private certainty that night still belonged to the girl he had caught in the air. Anri’s carried the fight video and the dead phone and the address in her pocket, the careful orbit of someone who had been allowed closer than she expected and was not ready to surrender the space.
Rio broke first, voice low enough that the stream of students past them became cover.
“Just so we’re clear,” she said. “Talking to him in class doesn’t mean you get him after. I’ve been in his nights since before you started saying Curtis-kun like that.”
Anri’s fingers tightened on her bag strap. She did not step back.
“I’m not trying to take something that isn’t mine.” Soft, but not yielding. “He saved me too. He answers when I call. He sent me his address so I wouldn’t have to search the whole city again. That isn’t nothing, Rio-san. And it isn’t only yours.”
Rio’s smirk flickered — not quite gone, not quite kind.
“Then say it properly. You’re claiming him.”
Anri’s face warmed. She still did not look away.
“…I’m saying I care about him. And I’m not stepping aside just because you got there first on a roof.” A small breath. “If he wants space, he gets it. If he wants company — I’m allowed to want to be the company.”
Rio held her gaze a second longer, then huffed a quiet laugh that did not reach friendly.
“Fine. Claim accepted. On both sides.” She tipped her chin toward the closed classroom door, toward the boy asleep through lunch. “He doesn’t get to sleep through the whole war, though. When he wakes up, he can figure out what to do with two girls who aren’t bluffing.”
They stood there in the hallway traffic with the same boy divided between a rooftop and a soft landing, neither of them knowing a dullahan had already rewritten his dark hours, and both of them finished with pretending the other was only a classmate.
The claim was on the table.
Curtis Nightstar was still asleep at his desk, owning none of the argument yet.
His phone vibrated against the desk.
Curtis surfaced enough to crack one mismatched eye open. Long black braids spilled over his arm as he dragged the new handset into view without sitting up properly — the classroom a soft blur of afternoon light and other people’s pencils. The name on the screen pulled the rest of him awake in a different way than Rio or Anri could.
Celty:
you alive
school surviving you or the other way around
last night is still in my head
He stared at the third line for a second longer than the first two.
The Namie debt, the suits on the pavement, the two girls claiming him in a hallway he had slept through — none of that belonged in a reply to a dullahan who had woken up in his bed and still did not know how wide the morning had already gone. He thumbed an answer with the slow care of someone half in a desk and half on a roof.
Curtis:
alive
mostly asleep at my desk like an idiot
school’s fine
last night’s in mine too
you ok?
He hit send, let the phone rest against his knuckles, and closed his eyes again for a breath.
In the hallway the claim was already spoken.
On a different street Namie Yagiri was still carrying an open call and a girl with a scar on her neck.
On his screen, Celty’s message sat warm and uncomplicated by any of it —
and Curtis Nightstar, late for nothing and early for too much, waited for a headless courier’s next words while the afternoon tried to steal him back to sleep.
Afternoon classes came and went.
Curtis surfaced for the ones that required a pen and drifted through the ones that did not, long black braids shifting whenever he tipped his head to keep from sliding under again. By the final bell the sleep debt had eased enough that he could stand without the room tilting. He gathered his things in the slow, practical order of someone who had already fought a morning on a sidewalk and spent lunch unconscious at a desk — bag, kit, phone face-down so Celty’s last message would not light up between two girls who did not own that file.
On his way out, Rio and Anri fell in on either side of him.
It was not an accident. The hallway claim still sat between them, unspoken now only because the boy himself was present. Rio took his left with the easy confidence of rooftops and night rides. Anri took his right with the quieter gravity of addresses sent in the dark and a soft Anri-chan before first period. For a stretch of corridor they walked like a problem that had decided to share the sidewalk.
“You’re actually vertical,” Rio said. “Progress.”
Anri glanced past him at Rio, then up at his face. “Did you sleep at all last night? You were out cold through lunch.”
Curtis adjusted the strap of his bag, mismatched eyes forward.
“Some,” he said, which was true and incomplete in the way most of his answers were. Celty’s helmet on a side table did not belong in the hallway. Namie’s open debt did not either. “I’m good. You two heading the same direction, or is this a full escort?”
Neither girl peeled off.
The campus gates drew closer. Students streamed around them. Somewhere in the flow Mikado watched the three-body shape of it and filed another quiet note. Masaomi, if he saw, would have opinions about darkness and soft landings and boys who collected complications without trying.
Rio’s voice stayed light. Anri’s stayed careful.
Curtis walked between the claim and the claim with a dullahan on his phone and a Yagiri sister in his debt, and the end of the school day felt less like freedom than the start of whatever night was about to demand next.
Curtis stopped just short of the gate and turned enough to take them both in — Rio on his left, Anri on his right, the hallway claim still humming under the polite surface.
“Listen.” Long black braids shifted against his uniform. “I’m actually busy this evening. I got some appointments I need to go to. I’m down to hang out later, whichever of you is free. Take care, both of you.”
It was gentle. It was final. It left no room to argue the appointments into a shared cafeteria table or a walk that turned into a claim in daylight.
Rio’s mouth tilted. Anri’s fingers flexed on her bag strap. For a second neither moved — then the practical fact of him already angling toward the street forced the release. They let him go.
Curtis slipped into the flow of students and was gone, bag over one shoulder, phone heavy with a dullahan’s messages and a morning that still owed Namie Yagiri nothing until he decided to collect.
Behind him, Rio and Anri looked at each other.
Same thought. Same timing. Neither said it out loud.
I’m getting to him first.
The gates kept spilling the rest of Raira into the afternoon. Somewhere past the crowd, Curtis Nightstar was already becoming a silhouette with appointments that might have been a courier, a debt, a roof, or simply the city — and two girls who had claimed him in a hallway were left with the same evening and the same quiet race neither of them intended to lose.
On the street Curtis smiled.
The Raira noise fell away behind him. Long black braids shifted as he dug a folded flyer from a pocket — something he had taken off a campus board without thinking too hard about it. The print was bright and a little desperate in the way student-club ads always were.
Build your own Gameboy!
Take home a free game!
Curtis huffed a quiet laugh under his breath.
“This is going to be pretty cool.”
He was still looking at the flyer when a car rolled up along the curb. Nice one. Dark, clean, the kind of quiet money that did not need to announce itself. The rear window lowered.
Namie Yagiri’s face found him like she had never left the morning sidewalk.
“Get in.”
Curtis whistled, low and appreciative, mismatched eyes flicking over the paint and the chrome before settling on her.
“Nice ride. But I don’t need an Uber.”
“It wasn’t a request.”
He tipped his head, braids sliding, the same almost-patient amusement he had worn when he put her men on the pavement.
“Touchy. But I’ll entertain you.”
He folded the Gameboy flyer once more, slipped it away, and got in.
The door shut on the street. The car pulled into traffic with the soft certainty of something that had been waiting for him since the open debt was accepted. Behind them, Raira kept emptying. Somewhere Rio and Anri were still carrying the same thought in opposite directions.
Inside, Namie did not look at him right away. When she did, the ice was back — thinner than the morning, edged with the scar on a girl’s neck and a boy who had walked to class holding her brother’s hours.
“Appointments,” she said, tasting the word he had given two classmates at the gate. “How convenient. You’re going to tell me more about the evenings, Curtis Nightstar. And you’re going to do it without an audience.”
Curtis settled into the seat, kit bag at his feet, the ghost of a smile still there.
The Gameboy club would have to wait.
Namie Yagiri would not.
The car took a quieter street.
Before Curtis could settle deeper into the seat, the man in front — not one of the two from the sidewalk, someone with cleaner hands and colder timing — twisted and put a gun against his head. The muzzle found the space above his ear with professional economy. Long black braids shifted as Curtis’s mismatched eyes tracked the weapon without otherwise moving.
Namie watched his face, not the gun.
“You walked into my morning, put my staff on the ground, priced my brother, and left me with a scar on a girl’s neck and an open call that could be anything.” Her voice stayed level, corporate ice over something sharper. “That makes you a liability. Liabilities get inventoried before they get trusted.”
The gun did not waver.
“So you are going to tell me — precisely — how much you know. About Seiji. About the evenings. About the girl. About why you already claimed to understand the trafficking. About what you are actually doing in this city with a Raira uniform and a smile.” A short pause. “If the inventory comes up short, or clever, or incomplete, this becomes a different conversation. One you do not walk to class from.”
The engine hummed. Outside, Ikebukuro continued not knowing a foreign student with braids was sitting in a Yagiri car with steel against his skull.
Namie’s eyes held his.
“Start talking, Curtis Nightstar. And do not entertain me. Inform me.”
Curtis smiled.
It was not the soft classroom smile and not the tired lunch smile. It was the one from the sidewalk — the one that had put a collar in his hand and an open debt on a Yagiri without raising his voice. Long black braids shifted against the seat as he tipped his head just enough that the muzzle followed the movement.
“Wow. Just wow.” Mismatched eyes found Namie’s over the gun, calm to the point of insult. “Just pull the trigger. I’m not scared of dying and I’m not scared of you.”
The car seemed to shrink around the sentence.
The man with the gun did not fire. His finger stayed disciplined, waiting on a nod that had not come. Namie’s face did not change in any large way — but something behind the ice recalculated in real time. Money had failed. Threats of death as currency had failed on the street. A literal gun to the head was failing in a leather seat with a foreigner who looked more disappointed than afraid.
“…You are serious,” she said quietly.
“I told you this morning.” Curtis’s smile did not fade. “Death is not leverage on me. You put steel on my skull and I still have your brother’s evenings and a girl with a scar on her neck. Pull it. Don’t pull it. The debt doesn’t move either way. You just look like someone who panics when a college kid won’t flinch.”
He let the silence sit.
Outside, the city kept moving. Inside, Namie Yagiri sat across from a boy who had just invited a bullet like it was a weather report, and understood — with a cold that had nothing to do with the air conditioning — that the liability she was trying to inventory might be the one person in Ikebukuro who could not be removed by ordinary means.
The gun stayed where it was.
The next word was still hers.
Curtis moved like the gun was interior decoration.
He fished a pack from his bag, tapped one free, and lit up in the leather quiet of her car. Smoke curled toward the ceiling. Long black braids shifted as he cracked the window just enough for manners and not enough for permission. Mismatched eyes found Namie over the ember.
“So what’s up with your brother?” The question landed casual, almost bored. “He in trouble? Why are you so hell-bent on finding him?”
The man with the gun glanced at Namie. She did not give him a nod. She did not give him a stand-down either. The muzzle stayed near Curtis’s head while the foreigner smoked in her back seat like he had booked the ride himself.
Namie watched the cigarette, then his face.
“You do not get to reverse the interview.”
Curtis exhaled toward the crack in the window. “You put a gun on me and asked how much I know. I’m asking why it matters this much. Same conversation. Different door.”
For a stretch of traffic the only sound was the soft rush of the street and the quiet burn of tobacco. When Namie spoke again, the corporate ice had thinned into something more personal and therefore more dangerous.
“Seiji is family.” Simple. Absolute. “He walked away from school. He walked away from the people who can keep him alive in this city. There are parties that would use him, sell him, or open him up for what our name is tied to. I am hell-bent because a brother who vanishes in Ikebukuro does not stay vanished by accident — and because every hour I do not know where he is, someone else might.”
Her eyes narrowed on the smoke.
“You claimed evenings. You claimed a girl with a scar on her neck. You claimed you already understood the trafficking. If you have seen him and you are sitting in my car smoking instead of delivering a location, then either you are protecting him… or you are enjoying the fact that I cannot find my own blood without a foreign student and an open debt.”
A short pause.
“Put the cigarette out when you are finished performing. Then decide whether you are going to be useful, or whether this gun becomes the least interesting part of your evening.”
Curtis took another drag, unhurried, the Gameboy flyer still folded in his pocket and a dullahan’s messages still waiting on a phone that did not belong in this car.
He had flipped the question.
Namie had answered enough to prove the brother was not only product — he was the nerve.
Curtis leaned in just enough.
Smoke left his mouth in a slow stream and found the man with the gun full in the face. The muzzle twitched. Long black braids shifted as Curtis’s mismatched eyes cut past the steel and locked on Namie instead.
“You’re lying to me.” Calm. Almost disappointed. “Otherwise you would not have let Seiji come to school the first day. Try again.”
The man did not wait for her order.
The gun came sideways in a short, professional arc and cracked against Curtis’s head — hard enough to snap his face toward the window, hard enough that the cigarette jumped from his fingers and died against the floor mat. The sound was ugly in the leather quiet. A thin line of blood found the edge of Curtis’s hairline and began its slow track down toward his jaw.
He did not fold.
He sat back against the seat, braids settling, and touched the spot with two fingers like he was checking the weather. When he looked at the man again there was no shock in it. Only the same flat inventory he had used on the sidewalk that morning.
Namie had not moved.
“Enough,” she said to the man — not rescue, correction. The gun returned to position near Curtis’s temple, steadier now, as if the hit had been punctuation and not a solution.
Curtis’s mouth tilted. Blood marked the tilt.
“That’s your answer?” he asked her, voice still even. “I call out the hole in the story and your peon hits me with the hardware? Seiji walked into Raira on day one. You had eyes. You had staff. You had every reason to pull him if the city was as lethal as you’re selling. So either the danger is newer than you’re admitting… or you’re not looking for a victim.” A short breath. “Try again, Namie. Without the fairy tale.”
The car rolled on.
Outside, Ikebukuro did not know a foreign student was bleeding quietly in a Yagiri back seat. Inside, Namie watched the blood and the refusal to flinch and understood the inventory was still incomplete — and that every time she reached for ordinary pressure, Curtis Nightstar treated it like weather he had already walked through.
Curtis laughed.
It was short, rough, and entirely wrong for a boy with fresh blood running from his hairline and a gun still near his temple. Long black braids shifted as he turned his head just enough to look the man in the eyes — not Namie this time. The man.
“And you.” The smile showed teeth. “Hit me again and I’ll shove that gun up your ass and pull the trigger.”
The muzzle twitched.
For a full second the only sound in the car was the engine and the soft wet track of blood finding Curtis’s jaw. The man’s professional calm developed a crack. He had hit people. He had been threatened. He had not often been threatened by the person he had just struck, in the same breath, with anatomical specificity and zero fear.
Namie’s voice cut the space before the man could decide whether pride required a second swing.
“Stand down.”
The gun eased back a fraction. Not away. Back.
She studied Curtis over the space between them — the laugh, the blood, the absolute lack of performance in the threat. Street punks had been a soft story for Anri. This was the version that had put two suits on pavement before first period and priced a brother without raising his voice.
“…You do not get to set terms while bleeding in my car,” she said.
Curtis wiped the blood with the side of his thumb and looked at it like it belonged to someone else.
“Then stop letting your furniture hit me when I catch you lying.” He tipped his chin at the man without looking away from her. “He swings again, I stop being polite about the inventory. Your choice.”
The threat hung in the leather quiet beside the unfinished story about Seiji and the first day of school.
Namie did not order another hit.
The man did not volunteer one.
Curtis Nightstar sat in the back seat of a Yagiri car, bleeding and amused, and waited to see whether the sister would try the truth a second time — or whether the evening was about to get a lot less conversational.
Namie held his gaze through the thin line of blood.
“Fine.” The word came out like a door closing on the man with the gun. “No more swings. You want a cleaner version — you get a cleaner version.”
She sat back against the leather, eyes never leaving Curtis’s face.
“Seiji came to school the first day because I allowed it. Because keeping him visible was safer than making him vanish on day one and painting a target on the absence. Raira is public. Public is messy. Messy is harder for the wrong people to own quietly.” A short pause. “That was the plan. Then he stopped answering. Then he stopped appearing. Then the evenings you claim to understand started mattering more than the classroom I could still see.”
Her voice stayed level. The personal edge under it did not.
“I am hell-bent because he is my brother and because our name does not get to lose family in Ikebukuro without someone collecting the pieces. You walked in with evenings. You walked in with a girl and a scar. You walked in refusing money and surviving a gun like it was weather. That makes you useful and it makes you dangerous — and I do not care which one you prefer to be as long as you stop treating my brother’s life like a game you get to score points in.”
She tipped her chin at the blood on his face.
“You are still bleeding in my car, Curtis Nightstar. You threatened my man with his own hardware. You called me a liar and you were not entirely wrong.” A thin beat. “So here is the trade without theater: you tell me what you actually know about the evenings and the girl. In return, I do not test whether your fearlessness survives a second conversation in a quieter place. Deal — or we keep discovering how little death scares you while my brother stays missing.”
The gun stayed present.
The city kept moving outside.
Namie waited for the foreign student with braids to decide whether the inventory would finally become mutual.
Curtis fished the pack out again.
The first cigarette had died on the floor mat when the gun found his head. He lit a second like the interruption had been a mild inconvenience, flame briefly painting the blood at his hairline. Smoke found the cracked window. Long black braids shifted as he settled deeper into the seat.
“Gonna burn through my pack.”
A short drag. Mismatched eyes on Namie over the ember.
“Well. You’re half telling the truth.” Calm. Almost conversational. “What’s the other half you won’t say?”
The man with the gun stayed still this time. Namie’s order held. The threat about the hardware sat in the air between them like a third passenger.
Namie watched the new cigarette with the same cold attention she had given the first.
Curtis kept going before she could close the door again.
“Visible at Raira. Safer than a quiet disappearance. Brother. Name. Evenings that started mattering more than the classroom.” He tipped the cigarette toward her, not accusing — inventorying. “That’s the half that sounds like family and logistics. The other half is the part where you care this much about a scar on a girl’s neck and why a foreign kid pricing Seiji in an open call makes you put steel on my skull instead of a business card. You’re not only looking for a brother who walked away. You’re looking for something that walked away with him — or something that took him. Try the second half, Namie. Without the brochure.”
Smoke curled toward the ceiling.
Outside, Ikebukuro did not know the Yagiri sister was being asked to finish a sentence she had spent years not finishing for strangers. Inside, Curtis Nightstar bled quietly, burned through another cigarette, and waited to see whether the debt would buy him the half she still had locked behind her teeth.
Curtis took another drag and did not look away.
“You have some sort of obsession with him?” The question landed soft and ugly at the same time. “Something taboo, perhaps?”
The temperature in the car dropped without the air conditioning changing.
Namie’s face did not explode. It went still in the particular way of someone who had just been touched in a place no stranger was supposed to know existed. The man with the gun flicked a glance at her and then very carefully looked at nothing. Long black braids framed Curtis’s calm, blood still marking the line from hairline to jaw, cigarette burning like he had asked about the weather.
For a stretch of street the only answer was the engine.
When Namie spoke, the corporate ice had cracks in it.
“…You do not get to use that word about my brother.” Quiet. Precise. Dangerous. “You do not get to sit in my car, bleed on my seats, and invent a pathology because I will not let this city swallow him. Family is not an obsession. Protecting him is not taboo. You are a foreign student with a bag full of tools and a talent for surviving things that should remove you from the board — that does not make you qualified to name what I feel.”
Her eyes held his.
“The second half is simpler than your imagination. Seiji is tied to things in this city that do not care about Raira schedules or soft landings. People who would use him. People who already have used pieces of what our name touches. I am hell-bent because the alternative is finding him when it is already too late to call it rescue.” A thin pause. “You want the rest of the inventory? Earn it. Tell me what you actually know about the evenings and the girl with the scar. Or keep fishing for taboo and discover how little patience I have left for psychology from a boy who lights cigarettes with a gun near his head.”
Curtis’s smoke found the cracked window.
He had pushed the nerve.
Namie had flinched without fleeing — and the debt was still open, waiting to see which half of the truth would cost more.
Curtis’s smile sharpened.
“There it is. Found you~”
Long black braids shifted as he tipped his head toward the man with the gun, blood still marking his hairline, cigarette burning between two fingers like punctuation.
“Hey, gunman. Shoot me now before I say what I’m going to say.”
Namie’s voice cut fast and hard.
“Don’t do it—”
Curtis did not look at her. He looked at the man.
“Aw, what’s wrong? Is the peon truly weak?” A soft, ugly chuckle. “Yes. Listen to her. You weak-minded insect.”
The man pulled the trigger.
The gun backfired.
The sound was wrong — a sharp, wet crack instead of a clean report — and the weapon came apart in the man’s grip like it had decided to hate him. Steel and powder and bone met in the same instant. His hand destroyed itself around the failure. He screamed. Blood hit the dash and the nice leather and the quiet money of the car.
Curtis exhaled smoke through his nose.
“Oh. Too bad. Maybe the smoke damaged your gun.”
He was already moving.
“Miss Yagiri. We can’t have the man bleeding in your nice car.”
Curtis went forward, grabbed the ruined peon by the collar and the ruined wrist, and put him out of the moving vehicle with the same economy he had used on the sidewalk that morning. The door, the street, the tumble — brief, brutal, done. Horns somewhere behind them. The city swallowed the scream without asking for a name.
Curtis climbed into the front seat, shut the world out again, and settled like he had booked the ride.
Cigarette. Blood. Braids. Mismatched eyes finding Namie in the sudden two-person quiet.
“Now you can tell me the other half you won’t say in front of anybody else.”
The car kept moving.
Outside, a man with a ruined hand learned the pavement. Inside, Namie Yagiri sat with an open debt, an empty gun, and a foreign student who had just turned her security into a story the street would not finish cleanly —
and the second half of the truth had nowhere left to hide.
For a stretch of street the only sound was the engine and the soft burn of Curtis’s cigarette.
Namie did not look back at the place where her man had hit the pavement. She looked at the foreigner now sitting in the front of her own car — blood at his hairline, braids settled, smoke curling like he had simply changed seats on a train. The gun was gone. The peon was gone. The ordinary tools she used on ordinary problems had just been turned into a mess the city would have to clean without her name on it.
When she spoke, the ice was thinner and the personal edge was no longer hiding.
“…You are not a student who got lucky on a sidewalk.” Quiet. Precise. “You are something that treats bullets and men like weather, and you just made my car a crime scene because a man followed an insult into a trigger pull.”
She exhaled once through her nose.
“The other half.” A short pause, as if she were still deciding how much of it he had already bought with blood and a ruined hand. “Seiji did not only walk away from school. He walked toward something that does not belong to Raira or to ordinary hours. There is a girl. There is a reason a scar on a neck matters more than a timetable. There are parties in this city who would open him up for what our family has touched — research, product, history — and I will not let them finish the work while I still have a board to move pieces on.”
Her eyes held his.
“You priced his evenings. You claimed to understand the trafficking. You sat through a gun and a hit and then threw my security into traffic without raising your pulse. So here is what I will say with no audience left: I need him found before someone else decides he is inventory instead of a brother. If you know the girl — if you know the evenings — you are either going to become useful, or you are going to become the next problem I have to solve without a peon in the front seat.”
A thin beat.
“And if you ever use the word taboo about him again in my hearing, Curtis Nightstar, I will test whether your fearlessness survives a quieter room than this car.”
Smoke found the cracked window.
Namie had given him more of the second half — girl, scar, research, parties who would open a Yagiri — without handing him the whole map. The debt was still open. The evening was still his appointment. And for the first time since the morning sidewalk, she was speaking to him like someone who might actually be dangerous enough to matter.
Curtis took another drag and did not soften it.
“It’s not normal for a sister to love her brother the way you do.” Smoke found the cracked window. Long black braids shifted as mismatched eyes held hers without apology. “I’m not stupid, Namie. Pretty up the words all you want. It’s not so much that he ran away. It’s who he ran away with. You want it to be you. Hence the word taboo.”
A short beat.
“I will talk how I please.”
The car seemed to lose air.
Namie’s face went through something that was not corporate and not clean — a flash of heat under the ice, the kind that lived in locked rooms and unfinished sentences about family. For a second it looked like she might reach for a new weapon, a new man, a quieter place after all. Then the control slammed back into place hard enough that the only thing that moved was her mouth.
“…You have a filthy little theory and a foreigner’s confidence,” she said, voice low and precise. “You throw a bleeding man into traffic, sit in my front seat, and decide you understand the shape of my blood. Seiji is my brother. What I feel is not a story you get to title. What he ran toward is not a romance you get to cast me in.”
Her eyes cut to the blood on his face, then back.
“You want the rest of the inventory? Here it is without the brochure: there is a girl he chose over every structure I built to keep him breathing. There is a reason a scar matters. There is a reason evenings matter. And yes — I want him back where I can reach him, where the city and its collectors cannot finish what they start. If that looks like obsession to a boy who lights cigarettes with guns near his head, that is your problem, not mine.”
A thin, dangerous pause.
“You will talk how you please. I will decide whether that tongue of yours stays useful or becomes another liability I remove without an audience. Choose carefully what you say next, Curtis Nightstar. You already cost me a man and a clean car. Do not cost yourself the only reason you are still sitting upright.”
Curtis’s smoke curled toward the ceiling.
He had named the nerve out loud.
Namie had neither confirmed nor fully denied it — only proven how close the word taboo sat to something she would kill to keep unnamed.
Curtis did not raise his voice.
He tipped his head toward the windshield, cigarette between two fingers, blood still drying in a thin line from hairline to jaw. Long black braids shifted against the seat.
“You do realize I can crash this car.” Calm. Almost helpful. “It will kill you. But I will probably walk away. Stop with the empty threats before you walk away with half a working brain.”
He exhaled smoke toward the cracked window.
The words sat in the leather quiet like a third body.
Namie’s hands did not move to the door. Her eyes did not flick to the street for a rescue that was no longer in the front seat. For a stretch of traffic she simply looked at him — at the foreign student who had survived a gun, a hit, a backfire, and the ejection of her peon, and was now pricing her life against his survival the same way he had priced her brother’s evenings before first period.
When she spoke, the threats thinned into something colder and more practical.
“…You would.” Not a question. An update to the inventory. “You would put us both into a wall and climb out of the wreckage because death is not leverage and pain is not a language you respect.”
A short breath.
“Fine. No more empty threats. You have made your point — repeatedly. You are not removable by ordinary means, and I am not going to waste another man discovering the ceiling of that fact tonight.” Her gaze held his. “So we are going to finish this conversation like adults who both still need something. You want the second half without the brochure. I want a direction on Seiji and the girl that is not theater. You crash this car and neither of us gets either.”
She tipped her chin at the cigarette.
“Talk, Curtis Nightstar. Or listen. But stop auditioning for the role of the thing that cannot be killed. I already believe you.”
Smoke curled toward the ceiling.
Namie had stepped back from the empty gun and the empty threats.
The debt was still open. The second half was still half-given. And Curtis Nightstar sat in the front seat of a Yagiri car with a crash in his pocket and a city that still did not know how much of him refused to die.
